      "id": 1,
      "text": "West Point was originally called Delaware, named after Governor Thomas West, 3rd Baron De La Warr, in 1705.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Point%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits where the Mattaponi and Pamunkey rivers meet to form the York River, separating Virginia Peninsula and Middle Peninsula.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Point%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "West Point was once the site of Cinquoteck, a Native American village of the Mattaponi, chief village of Opechancanough, brother of Chief Powhatan.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Point%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Richmond and York River Railroad first terminated in West Point, spurring its growth.",
